Yes, the proliferation problem is a serious one.  There are a half a dozen =
logical languages (in the present sense) around in various stages of develo=
pment (mostly early) and activity (mostly slight).  This new enterprise see=
ms to be driven less by the old "perfection being the enemy of the good" bu=
gbear and more by frustration with bureaucratic inertia (though, interestin=
gly, the first step in the project is to set up a new bureaucracy).  Person=
ally, though I am perfection-driven, I have decided that it is a better use=
 of time and talent (a lot of the one, a little of the other) to work with =
a nearly completed project in the hope that it will mange to get completed =
into a product that is good enough (does all the crucial things, even if in=
 sloppy, inefficient, even ugly, ways). =20


